WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.148
Laurie had just gotten home from school when Grandpa appeared, holding a tray of tea and cookies.

2
00:00:12.149 --> 00:00:17.326
"Welcome home, Laurie! You're just the girl I'm looking for.

3
00:00:17.327 --> 00:00:20.770
May I invite you to the study for a snack?"

4
00:00:20.771 --> 00:00:28.719
"Okay . . ." Laurie answered with a suspicious smile. What was Grandpa up to now?

5
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:34.466
"How do Japanese cookies and tea sound?" Grandpa asked, grinning.

6
00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.301
"Good."

7
00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:48.434
"Please follow me." Grandpa led the way upstairs and into the study.

8
00:00:48.435 --> 00:00:57.790
"Whoa! What's all this?" Laurie exclaimed. On the floor was a large, colorful map.

9
00:00:57.791 --> 00:01:04.198
Small buildings, ships, bridges, and figurines were scattered around it.

10
00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:11.923
"I need to attach all these bits and pieces to this map . . . by tomorrow! Can you help me?"

11
00:01:11.924 --> 00:01:17.896
"Sure!" Laurie replied. "What's it for?"

12
00:01:17.897 --> 00:01:24.801
"I'm presenting a unit at the high school on the social order of Japan during the Tokugawa period—from

13
00:01:24.802 --> 00:01:33.283
1600 to 1867—when Japan was ruled by the powerful Tokugawa family. I'm

14
00:01:33.284 --> 00:01:38.537
going to use this map of Japan to show where things took place." Grandpa

15
00:01:38.538 --> 00:01:41.777
picked up a small wooden bridge. "For

16
00:01:41.778 --> 00:01:49.310
example, we'll use this to mark the capital, Edo, which is now called Tokyo. Edo

17
00:01:49.311 --> 00:01:57.151
was a busy place; merchants, craftsmen, and lots of samurai crossed over this bridge. So

18
00:01:57.152 --> 00:02:00.943
we'll need to put those figures around here." "Samurai

19
00:02:00.944 --> 00:02:07.372
were warriors," Laurie stated. "Why were there so many of them?" "Because

20
00:02:07.373 --> 00:02:13.511
the shogun—the top leader in Japan at the time—lived in Edo. He

21
00:02:13.512 --> 00:02:18.212
was a warlord who held even more power than the emperor. This

22
00:02:18.213 --> 00:02:24.409
is his castle, actually," Grandpa said, picking up a castle replica. "The

23
00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:33.196
shogun required that samurai lords from all across Japan live in Edo for part of every year. That

24
00:02:33.197 --> 00:02:37.146
was his way of keeping an eye on them." "This

25
00:02:37.147 --> 00:02:40.404
is a samurai, right?" Laurie asked. She

26
00:02:40.405 --> 00:02:43.686
held up a man carrying a sword. "Yep.

27
00:02:43.687 --> 00:02:51.383
Samurai could easily be recognized by their armor, swords, and hairstyles. This

28
00:02:51.384 --> 00:02:55.322
one was probably a middle-ranking samurai. He

29
00:02:55.323 --> 00:03:01.330
would have been employed by one of the samurai lords, who were landowners. For

30
00:03:01.331 --> 00:03:07.467
centuries there was no central government to keep Japan stable. So

31
00:03:07.468 --> 00:03:14.038
there were constant civil wars between lords to expand their land and power. Samurai

32
00:03:14.039 --> 00:03:20.324
were not only highly respected, they were considered necessary! The

33
00:03:20.325 --> 00:03:27.288
warrior samurai and their lords made up the highest social class in Japan back then." Laurie

34
00:03:27.289 --> 00:03:34.743
picked up a figure of a skinny man wearing a triangular straw hat. "Where does this one go?" "He's

35
00:03:34.744 --> 00:03:41.396
a rice farmer. We'll put him in the farmlands surrounding Edo. Farmers

36
00:03:41.397 --> 00:03:44.849
were in the next highest class. They

37
00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:50.065
were important because they took care of the crops that fed the nation. Rice

38
00:03:50.066 --> 00:03:56.180
was, and still is, the main food eaten by the Japanese. During

39
00:03:56.181 --> 00:04:03.804
the Tokugawa period, rice was so valuable that it was used to pay rent! "This

40
00:04:03.805 --> 00:04:11.853
is a craftsman. Since he's holding this small bowl, he probably makes lacquerware." Grandpa

41
00:04:11.854 --> 00:04:16.673
pointed to the tray he had brought up with the tea and cookies. "This

42
00:04:16.674 --> 00:04:25.562
is Japanese lacquerware. The Japanese carved items out of wood and then coated them with a shiny gloss. They

43
00:04:25.563 --> 00:04:30.661
perfected this art on their bowls and boxes and things. Anyway,

44
00:04:30.662 --> 00:04:38.700
this craftsman would be in the third social class, along with weavers, sword makers, and carpenters . . . "That

45
00:04:38.701 --> 00:04:43.343
guy you're holding would probably fit into the last class." "It

46
00:04:43.344 --> 00:04:48.073
looks like he's carrying lots of stuff," Laurie said. "He's

47
00:04:48.074 --> 00:04:56.488
a merchant. In ancient and medieval Japan, merchants were at the bottom of the social hierarchy. In

48
00:04:56.489 --> 00:05:05.338
a culture that valued hard work, making money off someone else's labor wasn't as respectable as doing the labor yourself. But

49
00:05:05.339 --> 00:05:13.173
people began realizing that merchants were a key part of the economy, and merchants' value grew. Without

50
00:05:13.174 --> 00:05:17.715
them, cities might not have developed so quickly." Laurie

51
00:05:17.716 --> 00:05:23.123
nodded as she added a cherry tree near the shogun's castle. "This

52
00:05:23.124 --> 00:05:30.358
medieval Japanese society had a rigid class system, but it was also peaceful. The

53
00:05:30.359 --> 00:05:38.336
civil wars ended when the Tokugawa family claimed control over all the other warlords. Big

54
00:05:38.337 --> 00:05:44.135
cities developed and prospered; universities were built for high-ranking samurai; and

55
00:05:44.136 --> 00:05:51.740
arts like poetry, literature, painting, and lacquerware expressed Japanese culture. "The

56
00:05:51.741 --> 00:06:00.026
Tokugawa shogun thought the best way to preserve this peace and stability was to close Japan off from other countries, particularly

57
00:06:00.027 --> 00:06:07.819
Western countries, which had a history of creating Christian empires and trading colonies. So

58
00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:11.713
foreigners weren't usually allowed in Japan." "Then

59
00:06:11.714 --> 00:06:15.683
who's this guy? He doesn't look Japanese!" Laurie

60
00:06:15.684 --> 00:06:20.809
exclaimed, picking up a white man dressed in a military uniform. "Ha!

61
00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:27.116
You found him! That's Commodore Perry," Grandpa said. "Despite

62
00:06:27.117 --> 00:06:35.843
Japan's wishes, the United States really wanted Japan to open its ports to their traders and sailors. In

63
00:06:35.844 --> 00:06:42.928
1853 they sent Perry over with four armed, black naval ships. They

64
00:06:42.929 --> 00:06:48.637
warned the Japanese that if ports weren't opened, they would fire!" "What

65
00:06:48.638 --> 00:06:51.671
did the Japanese do then?" "Intimidated

66
00:06:51.672 --> 00:07:03.297
by the guns and steamships—which were new to the Japanese—they signed an agreement with the U.S. After that things changed rapidly. In 1867

67
00:07:03.298 --> 00:07:09.416
the shogun resigned, and the emperor suddenly died. Then a

68
00:07:09.417 --> 00:07:13.612
young emperor called Meiji came to power. Emperor Meiji

69
00:07:13.613 --> 00:07:22.569
realized that if Japan wanted to keep itself safe in the modern world, it would need Western technology. So the

70
00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:30.835
Japanese imported ideas about education, politics, and science from the United States and Europe. But this

71
00:07:30.836 --> 00:07:34.725
map only covers the Tokugawa era. What do

72
00:07:34.726 --> 00:07:37.762
you say—are we finished?" Laurie scanned

73
00:07:37.763 --> 00:07:45.837
the map, now speckled with people and buildings. "I think so," she replied. "Tokugawa Japan

74
00:07:45.838 --> 00:07:54.486
looks like it might have been an interesting place to visit . . . except I wouldn't have been allowed to enter the country!" Grandpa chuckled.

75
00:07:54.487 --> 00:08:03.604
"You're absolutely right. Well, thank you, Laurie. I couldn't

76
00:08:03.605 --> 00:08:06.164
have done it without you. Now how

77
00:08:06.165 --> 00:08:11.044
about our snack?" he said. "In Japan,

78
00:08:11.045 --> 00:08:16.000
before you begin eating, you say itadakimasu! So on

79
00:08:16.001 --> 00:08:20.901
the count of three, ready? One, two, three—" " Itadakimasu

80
00:08:20.902 --> 00:08:30.945
!" they both cried and reached for their cookies.

